  "FcLeakText": "The random row split gave the highest balanced accuracy, 0.915, against 0.898 on unseen gauges in later years, and the two bootstrap intervals do not overlap. Random splitting inflated balanced accuracy by 1.7 points and PR-AUC by 7.4 points. PR-AUC ranged from 0.724 (new years) to 0.810 (random rows). Holding out later years lowered PR-AUC more than holding out gauges (0.724 against 0.789), so a temporal split matters at least as much as a spatial one. In the spatial protocol, the test years are also the years from which each gauge's flood threshold was computed.",
  "FcLeakDiscuss": "the evaluation protocol alone moved balanced accuracy by 1.7 points (Table~\\ref{tab:leak}), so scores from random row splits overstate what a forecaster will achieve at a new gauge, and published Indian flood models can only be compared under the same split.",
  "FcLeakConclude": "A random row split would have reported a balanced accuracy 1.7 points higher.",
  "FcLeakAbstract": "Scoring the same model on random rows raised balanced accuracy to 0.915, an inflation of 1.7 points.",
